<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1863 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">When Johnny Comes Marching Home</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from the American Civil War about the happy day a soldier comes home and the whole town turns out to cheer.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Patrick Gilmore (as &quot;Louis Lambert&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional; the tune noted down and arranged by Patrick Gilmore</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Americans of the Civil War, Union and Confederate</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Boston, Massachusetts, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1863 (deposited in the Library of Congress on 26 September 1863)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Patrick Gilmore, published 1863 under the name &quot;Louis Lambert&quot;)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/when-johnny-comes-marching-home">When Johnny Comes Marching Home</a>&quot; was written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> by the Irish-American bandleader Patrick Gilmore. Its first edition was deposited in the Library of Congress on 26 September 1863, with words and music credited to &quot;Louis Lambert&quot;; the publisher, Henry Tolman &amp; Co. of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, kept the copyright. Why Gilmore used a pen name is unclear, though songwriters of the time often did so to give their songs a touch of romantic mystery. He is said to have written it for his sister Annie, who prayed for the safe return of her fiancé, the Union Light Artillery captain John O'Rourke; it is not clear that they were already engaged in 1863, and they married only in 1875.</p><p>Gilmore did not claim the tune. In an 1883 article in the Musical Herald he called it &quot;a musical waif&quot; he had heard someone humming early in the war, which he wrote down, dressed up, named and &quot;rhymed into usefulness&quot; for the times. The melody had been printed around 1 July 1863 for the Civil War drinking song &quot;Johnny Fill Up the Bowl&quot;, whose sheet music says it was arranged, not composed, by J. Durnal, and an undated slip of Gilmore's words from his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> publisher tells singers to use that tune. &quot;Johnny Fill Up the Bowl&quot; was a topical song that soldiers and publishers kept refitting with new words; one satirical version, &quot;For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bales</a>&quot;, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 1864 by A. E. Blackmar, mocked the alleged thieving of some Union soldiers there. The tune has been likened to that of &quot;John Anderson, My Jo&quot;, which goes back to about 1630 or earlier, and Jonathan Lighter has suggested a link to the 17th-century ballad &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a>&quot;. The song is often thought to be a rewriting of &quot;Johnny I Hardly Knew Ye&quot;, but that song was not published until 1867 and first had a different melody.</p><p>The song voiced the longing of families for the return of men away at the war, and it was sung on both sides, Union and Confederate. Gilmore's words were printed in 1863 in Erastus Beadle's Beadle's Dime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Book, No. 15. The song became a hit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Ireland as well, where it was eventually rewritten into &quot;Johnny I Hardly Knew Ye&quot;. Its tune carried new words for new causes: in the presidential campaign of 1880, &quot;If the Johnnies Get into Power&quot; backed the Republicans James A. Garfield and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> A. Arthur against the Democrats Winfield S. Hancock and William H. English, and in 1914 a British version appeared as &quot;When Tommy Comes Marching Home&quot;.</p><p>It became popular again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Glenn Miller recorded it in 1942 with Tex Beneke, Marion Hutton and The Modernaires, and Guy Lombardo and the Andrews Sisters recorded it the same year, when Morton Gould also set it as &quot;American Salute&quot;, a set of variations that became a staple of American orchestral music. In John Ford's western The Horse Soldiers the last line was changed into a promise to &quot;raise hell&quot;, and the tune and refrain live on in children's songs such as &quot;The Ants Go Marching&quot;, which Robert D. Singleton adapted for the Barney special &quot;Campfire Sing-Along&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of homecoming: a town imagines the day its soldier returns from the war, and the cheering and celebration that will greet him. The mood is brisk and joyful, though it is a hope voiced in wartime, before anyone knows who will come back.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Johnny comes marching home again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll give him a hearty welcome then,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The men will cheer, the boys will shout,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The ladies they will all turn out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we'll all feel gay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Johnny comes marching home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The old church bell will peal with joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To welcome our darling boy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The village lads and lassies say</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With roses they will strew the way,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we'll all feel gay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Johnny comes marching home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Get ready for the Jubilee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll give the hero three times three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The laurel wreath is ready now</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To place upon his loyal brow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we'll all feel gay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Johnny comes marching home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let love and friendship on that day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their choicest treasures then display,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And let each one perform some part</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To fill with joy the warrior's heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we'll all feel gay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Johnny comes marching home.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Louis Lambert (Patrick Gilmore), 1863; from O. H. Oldroyd, The Good Old Songs We Used to Sing, '61 to '65 (Washington, 1902). Text from <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/21566" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="bJCFumLzt5U" data-title="When Johnny Comes Marching Home: The Mormon Tabernacle Choir, cond. Richard P. Condie, arr. Peter J. Wilhousky (Songs of the Civil War, Sony Classical, 1961); choir, sung"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Mormon Tabernacle Choir, cond. Richard P. Condie, arr. Peter J. Wilhousky (Songs of the Civil War, Sony Classical, 1961); choir, sung</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bJCFumLzt5U" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">For Bales</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1864</span><span class="sg-chain-h">satirical words to the same tune (a variant of &quot;Johnny Fill Up the Bowl&quot;)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny I Hardly Knew Ye</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1867</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rewriting; sung to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">If the Johnnies Get into Power</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1880</span><span class="sg-chain-h">campaign song, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">When Tommy Comes Marching Home</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1914</span><span class="sg-chain-h">British version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">American Salute (Morton Gould)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">orchestral variations on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glenn Miller, with Tex Beneke, Marion Hutton and The Modernaires</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Guy Lombardo and the Andrews Sisters</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Ants Go Marching</span><span class="sg-chain-h">children's song, same tune and refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Animals Went in Two by Two</span><span class="sg-chain-h">children's song, same tune and refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny Comes Marching Home (Adam Faith, arranged by John Barry)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">film theme and single</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English Civil War (Johnny Comes Marching Home) (The Clash)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reworded version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Na Bole Tum, Na Maine Kuch Kaha (film Baton Baton Mein)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1979</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny Reviens d'la Guerre (Bérurier Noir)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1983</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Civil War (Guns N' Roses)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1991</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody whistled in the intro and outro</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny Fill Up the Bowl</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">John Anderson, My Jo</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1630</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melodic resemblance</span></li><li><a href="/songs/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">17th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible connection (Jonathan Lighter)</span></li></ul></section>
